WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:09.170
Yes, release the Kraken!

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.870
Recognizable by its massive size and tentacled
limbs, the Kraken is one of the most legendary

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.321
and feared sea monsters of the deep.

00:00:16.321 --> 00:00:20.640
Stories of the Kraken say it could take down
whole ships, grab sailors off decks, and create

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.400
whirlpools—all to get humans into the water
and into the monster’s waiting beak.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.060
Because despite having more than half of the
world’s species to choose from for dinner,

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.160
apparently there is nothing in the ocean more
delicious than humans.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.590
We know less about the oceans on our own planet
than we do about our solar system.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:50.150
And before we started going to space, sailing
far away from land was just about the scariest,

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:53.080
most uncertain trip a person could take.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.059
So much about the ocean is inherently terrifying.

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:58.300
It’s dark, vast, deep, and unpredictable.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:02.690
It sparks our imaginations of what could lie
beyond the horizon, or what lurks beneath

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:04.660
the waves.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:12.780
And that’s where the Kraken comes in.

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:14.180
The first written mention of the colossal

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:18.110
sea beast was by King Svarah of Norway in
1180.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:23.080
He tells sailors to look out for a large squid-like
monster that swims the coasts of Norway, Greenland,

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.080
and Iceland.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.630
Since then, the Kraken has continuously appeared
in literature, most famously in the work of

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.390
Jules Verne, Victor Hugo, and Alfred Tennyson.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.680
It’s not surprising that we first find this

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.530
creature in Northern Europe given the reliance
the Nordic people had on the ocean for trade,

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:41.260
travel, and survival.

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:45.980
Unlike other sea monsters that require a bit
more imagination, the idea of a giant, tentacled

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:50.120
creature prowling below the surface of the
water, waiting to take down unsuspecting ships

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.270
and sailors makes sense.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:56.810
Because there are living organisms that fit
the description (minus the sailor-eating part).

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.700
The giant squid and the colossal squid are

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.729
two living cephalopods that can be found in
every ocean on Earth.

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:06.140
Giant squid really are giant, measuring up
to 43 feet in length.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.670
But the colossal squid holds the title of
world’s largest living invertebrate, growing

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:13.590
up to 49 feet long (at current record!).

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.340
The largest one found so far weighed over
1,000 pounds.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:21.650
Oh, and in addition to suckers, it has barbed
hooks on its tentacles, and they are very

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:22.980
fast swimmers.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.260
Both species frequent the deep, cold waters
of the ocean, making it hard to see them in

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.790
their natural habitat.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:32.600
Before the 21st-century, no photographic evidence
of a living giant squid existed.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:36.980
It wasn’t until 2012 that we first caught
these creatures alive on video in their natural

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:37.980
habitat.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.819
The Kraken is bigger than giant and larger
than colossal.

00:02:39.819 --> 00:02:44.660
It’s a sea beast with multiple tentacles
that often acts aggressively towards humans.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.690
A Kraken is basically an angrier, much larger
version of a squid.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.380
Personally, I’m terrified of the Kraken.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.320
Something about those giant eyes, wiggling
tentacles, and suckers just completely freaks

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:57.320
me out.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:58.560
And I’m not the only one.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.410
Victor Hugo apparently hated anything with

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:01.410
tentacles.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:06.040
In his 1866 novel The Toilers of the Sea,
a character has a frightening encounter with

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:10.349
an octopus who wraps itself around his body
and almost kills him.

00:03:10.349 --> 00:03:15.130
Hugo dedicates an entire chapter outside of
the plot to rant about how terrifying and

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:17.620
monstrous these quote “Devil-Fish” are.

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:22.350
Writing about quote, “what ancient legends
call the Krakens,” he describes them as

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.410
“glutinous masses endowed with a malignant
will.”

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.550
Hugo describes how such a creature could wrap

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:33.230
itself around a swimmer and drown them, and
even goes as far as to say they are capable

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:35.150
of sucking blood with their tentacles.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:40.050
He summarizes his fear of suckers stating,
“Claws are harmless compared with the horrible

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:41.600
action of these natural cupping-glasses.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.420
The talons of the wild beast enter into your
flesh; but with the cephalopod, it is you

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:48.850
who enter into the creature.”

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:49.780
Yikes.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:54.480
There are real accounts of octopuses attacking
and even drowning swimmers, many of which

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:59.959
occur in the 19th century, the same time Hugo,
Tennyson, and Verne wrote their Kraken stories.

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:04.099
In Verne’s Twenty Thousand Leagues Under
the Sea, Captain Nemo and his crew are attacked

00:04:04.099 --> 00:04:09.620
by not just one, but “10 or 12,” “horrible
monsters” described as giant octopuses.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:14.520
It’s a bloody, inky, chaotic fight scene
with tentacles flailing everywhere, and the

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.310
crew defending itself with hatchets and harpoons.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:22.079
Tennyson’s 1862 poem “The Kraken” depicts
the monster as an apocalyptic force that will

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:27.930
rise from quote “His ancient, dreamless,
uninvaded sleep” one day to be seen by “men

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:32.029
and angels.”The Kraken appears in nonfiction
literature as well, with reports of giant

00:04:32.029 --> 00:04:34.479
squid corpses beginning in the 14th century.

00:04:34.479 --> 00:04:39.069
We have accounts of massive tentacles washing
ashore and of giant squid corpses floating

00:04:39.069 --> 00:04:40.090
at sea.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:44.270
Sperm whales are the natural predator of giant
squid, so pieces of the creatures have been

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:49.210
found in their stomachs, and they even sometimes
get scars of huge sucker marks from a squid

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:50.959
that put up a fight.

00:04:50.959 --> 00:04:52.469
Okay, but how does that explain the legends

00:04:52.469 --> 00:04:54.439
of Kraken attacking humans?

00:04:54.439 --> 00:04:58.219
Well, octopuses have attacked swimmers and
divers—we have the footage.

00:04:58.219 --> 00:05:01.520
But there’s another element at work—sharks.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.620
We need to take these predators into account
when talking about the kraken.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.639
Though shark attacks are rare, they do happen.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:12.650
Often, all a witness would see is a flailing
body, blood, and maybe even the victim dragged

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:17.430
under the surface of the water—but no evidence
of what was causing the attack.

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:21.330
This established the idea that large creatures
in the ocean posed a threat to humans.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:25.529
Combine that with sightings of the alien body
of a giant squid or a large tentacle, and

00:05:25.529 --> 00:05:27.560
the Kraken legend begins to take form.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.220
To quote Hugo: “These animals are indeed
phantoms as much as monsters.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:33.580
They are proved and yet improbable.”

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:36.939
The Kraken is unnerving because it resembles

00:05:36.939 --> 00:05:37.949
a real animal.

00:05:37.949 --> 00:05:42.569
Sure, we don’t have conclusive evidence
of one over 50 feet long, but we’ve only

00:05:42.569 --> 00:05:45.800
measured around 500 giant squid throughout
recorded history.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:49.340
We can hardly call that a representative sample
of an entire species.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.590
All we really have are glimpses of a creature

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:53.169
we don’t completely understand.

00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:57.599
The relatively few species we do know about
in the deep ocean are frightening because

00:05:57.599 --> 00:05:58.599
they seem otherworldly.

00:05:58.599 --> 00:06:03.630
We don’t need to look to space for aliens;
they exist right here in the uncharted territories

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:04.759
of Earth.

00:06:04.759 --> 00:06:09.060
Even more bizarre creatures may exist in the
dark depths of our oceans, inspiring authors

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:13.000
to craft even stranger, more terrifying sea
monsters.

